> **OPT-IN RULE — not globally active.** This standard applies **only** in projects whose `.flow/project.md` lists it in its `## standards` section — as the bare filename `kotlin-backend-development-standard.mdc`, which resolves to `<agents repo>/rules/<name>` per **Project configuration** in `skills/flow-contracts/project-configuration.md`. It is deliberately **not** installed as an always-on global rule: its Kotlin globs (`src/**/*.kt`, `**/*.kts`) would otherwise match Compose Multiplatform frontends and IntelliJ/IDE plugin repositories, instructing them to adopt a Spring backend layering (`core/domain`, `core/ports`, `infrastructure/persistence`) that is wrong for those project shapes. If the current project has not opted in, ignore this file and impose none of its layering requirements.

# Kotlin Backend Development Standard

> **Living document.** This standard evolves with the team and ecosystem. Improvement proposals are welcome via PR.

## 1. Purpose and principles

- **Consistency**: Every service module is organized predictably.
- **Pragmatism**: The standard solves real team problems; it must not create bureaucracy.
- **Evolution**: The document may change when better practices or technologies emerge.

Follow [JetBrains Kotlin coding conventions](https://kotlinlang.org/docs/coding-conventions.html). `./gradlew ktlintCheck detekt` must pass with zero violations.

## 2. Gymie Gradle layout

| Path | Module | Role |
|------|--------|------|
| `src/app/` | `:app` | Composition root, Flyway migrations, Boot entry point |
| `src/gateway/` | `:gateway` | Public edge — routing, JWT validation, CORS |
| `src/app/<domain>/` | `:auth`, … | Domain library — `core` + `infrastructure` below |
| `src/app/common/` | `:common` | Shared cross-cutting types and filters |

## 3. Domain module layout (`core` + `infrastructure`)

Every domain Gradle module (e.g. `:auth`) splits **all** code into two top-level packages under `com.gymie.<module>`:

```
com/gymie/auth/
├── core/                                    # business logic ONLY
│   ├── domain/                              # domain models (data class / sealed class)
│   │   └── <domainName>/
│   │       └── specification/               # query/response shapes for this domain
│   ├── exceptions/                          # business exceptions from core services
│   ├── ports/                               # outbound interfaces core depends on
│   │   ├── persistence/
│   │   │   └── <domainName>/                # e.g. UserRepository.kt (interface)
│   │   ├── jwt/                             # e.g. AccessTokenIssuer
│   │   ├── token/                           # refresh, email verification, password reset stores
│   │   └── security/                        # e.g. PasswordHasher
│   └── services/
│       └── <serviceName>/
│           ├── <Service>.kt                 # interface
│           └── impl/
│               └── <Service>Impl.kt         # implementation
└── infrastructure/                          # everything non-business
    ├── common/
    ├── configurations/                      # Spring @Configuration beans
    ├── integrations/                        # port implementations for external systems
    │   ├── redis/
    │   ├── jwt/
    │   └── …
    ├── metrics/
    ├── persistence/                         # DB adapters, DAOs, entities, converters
    │   ├── dao/
    │   ├── entities/                        # when split from core domain
    │   └── converters/
    ├── presentation/                        # inbound user-facing entry points
    │   └── web/
    │       ├── dto/
    │       ├── exceptions/
    │       ├── validators/
    │       ├── converters/
    │       └── security/                    # filters, OAuth handlers for HTTP
    └── scheduler/
```

### Dependency rules

- **`core`** operates on `core.domain` models and **`core.ports`** interfaces only.
- **`core.services`** may depend only on `core` packages (`domain`, `exceptions`, `ports`, other `services` interfaces).
- **`infrastructure`** implements `ports`, hosts HTTP/gRPC, DB, caches, schedulers, and Spring wiring.
- **`infrastructure.presentation`** converts DTOs ↔ domain before/after calling `core.services`.
- **`infrastructure.configurations`** wires beans; no business logic.

```
presentation/web → core.services → core.ports ← infrastructure.persistence / integrations
                      ↓
                 core.domain
```

Do **not** use top-level `application/`, `api/`, `dto/`, `config/`, or legacy `service/` packages in domain modules.

## 4. Core — `domain/`

- All domain models live here — usually `data class`, sometimes `sealed class`.
- Each domain concept gets its own subpackage (e.g. `core/domain/user/User.kt`).

### `domain/.../specification/`

Use when:

- A response combines multiple domains or selected fields.
- A service/port method needs typed query parameters.

Name types clearly; keep them in `core`, not in `infrastructure`.

## 5. Core — `exceptions/`

All business exceptions thrown from `core.services` belong here. Map them to HTTP in `infrastructure.presentation.web.exceptions`.

## 6. Core — `ports/`

Every outbound dependency of `core.services` exposes an interface here, grouped by integration (e.g. `ports/persistence/user/UserRepository.kt`).

- Persistence ports: `ports/persistence/<domainName>/`
- JWT ports: `ports/jwt/` (e.g. `AccessTokenIssuer`)
- Token/session ports: `ports/token/` (refresh store, email verification, password reset)
- Security ports: `ports/security/` (e.g. `PasswordHasher`)
- Other external system ports: `ports/<systemName>/`

**Core must not import Spring, JPA, Redis, HTTP, or JWT types.**

## 7. Core — `services/`

- One package per service: interface at package root, implementation in `impl/`.
- Implementation may use `@Service` / `@Component` when wired by Spring.
- Services depend on **ports**, not concrete infrastructure classes.

## 8. Infrastructure

When implementing ports or serving requests, use **core domain types** at boundaries. Provide reusable `converters` and `validators` under the relevant infrastructure subtree.

| Subpackage | Role |
|------------|------|
| `configurations/` | `@Configuration`, filter-chain wiring, DI grouping facades |
| `persistence/dao/` | Spring Data JPA (or other) DAO interfaces |
| `persistence/` | Adapters implementing `core.ports.persistence` |
| `integrations/<tech>/` | Redis, JWT library, external HTTP/gRPC clients |
| `presentation/web/` | REST controllers, DTOs, exception handlers, security filters |
| `scheduler/` | Cron / scheduled jobs |
| `metrics/` | Module-specific metrics (prefer `:common/observability` when cross-cutting) |

## 9. Tests

Mirror `core/` and `infrastructure/` paths in test source sets. Layout is enforced by `BaselineLayoutTest` in `:app`.

## 10. Agent checklist

1. Business logic? → `core/services/<name>/impl/`
2. Domain model? → `core/domain/`
3. Business error? → `core/exceptions/`
4. Outbound contract? → `core/ports/`
5. DB / cache / JWT / HTTP / config? → matching `infrastructure/` subtree
6. Run `./gradlew ktlintCheck detekt :auth:test :app:test`

Reference: `README.md`, `src/app/auth/README.md`
